>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `add_looper`

``` hol4
simpLib.add_looper :
  string * (simpset -> tactic) -> simpset -> simpset
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Adds a named looper to a simpset.

A looper is a tactic that the simplification tactics apply to a goal
that rewriting and the final solvers leave open; see
[`simpLib.GEN_SIMP_TAC`](#simpLib.GEN_SIMP_TAC). In
`add_looper (name, mk) ss`, `mk` receives the simpset of the current
call and returns the tactic. Loopers are tried in the order they were
added. A new `name` is appended; an existing one has its tactic
replaced in place.

The tactic must fail when it does not apply (`Conv.UNCHANGED` counts as
failure) and make progress when it succeeds. `ExclX ("looper:" ^ name)`
in a theorem list disables the looper for that call. Bare `ExclX name`
first uses the strict rewrite-name parser and falls through to strategy
targets only when no rewrite or decision procedure matches; see
[`simpLib.ExclX`](#simpLib.ExclX).

### Failure

Never fails.

### Example

``` hol4
>> val c = Context.snapshot();
>>_ val conj_ss = simpLib.add_looper ("conj", fn _ => CONJ_TAC) bool_ss;
>> SIMP_TAC conj_ss [] ([], “p ∧ q”) c;
>> SIMP_TAC conj_ss [simpLib.ExclX "looper:conj"] ([], “p ∧ q”) c;
```

